{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, these catalytic domains belong to separate families within the same Actin-like ATPase domain superfamily, c.55.1 (SUNID 53067): Actin/HSP70 is c.55.1.1 (53068), hexokinase is c.55.1.3 (53083), and glycerol kinase is c.55.1.4 (53089). Their superfamily lies within the Ribonuclease H-like motif fold, c.55 (53066), in the alpha/beta class, c (51349).

Shared superfamily membership supports remote common ancestry of the classified domains despite substantial sequence and functional divergence. It supports an evolutionarily related structural core and ATP-associated catalytic architecture. This is stronger than sharing a fold, which alone does not establish homology; class membership describes broad secondary-structure organization. Family membership indicates a closer relationship, but even the shared Actin/HSP70 family does not imply identical cellular functions.

Actin couples nucleotide turnover to cytoskeletal behavior, HSP70 uses ATP hydrolysis in chaperone cycles, hexokinase transfers phosphate from ATP to a hexose, and glycerol kinase phosphorylates glycerol. Neither these specific functions nor substrate specificity, kinetics, regulation, or interaction partners can be transferred across the superfamily from its name alone. The relationship applies to the classified domains, not automatically to every domain of the complete proteins. A distant member can inform core structural modeling, but reliable active-site or functional inference requires sequence and structural alignment, conserved catalytic residues, ligand evidence, and biochemical validation; a representative from one family cannot stand in for all families’ functional properties.","evidence_used":["Pretrained model knowledge","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=actin","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067","/api/v1/openapi.yaml","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/parents"]}]}
